  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 442 AM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nLong range model solutions depict a prevailing wetter pattern as \nmoisture and instability continue to surge during the rest of the\nweekend and into early next week. Winds on Sunday and Monday will\ncontinue to transition from southwesterly to southerly winds, \nthen becoming easterly by Tuesday as the W Atl ridge expands \ncloser to SoFlo. This will shift the best chances of rain from \neastern/interior areas during the weekend to western areas by mid \nweek. \n\nLatest NBM has significantly surged in moisture advection and \ncontent across SoFlo, lingering through the rest of the work week. \nModel PWAts well above 2\" and max POPs upwards of 90% will keep a \nvery active weather pattern, with wettest days being Sunday and \nMonday attm. The added instability and upper-lvl support from \npassing short-wave impulses along the base of the trough aloft will \nincrease the potential for strong storms on both days, especially\naround the Lake region. \n\nBy Tuesday, easterly flow re-establishes and a more typical summer \ntime pater will return with sea breezes driving convection towards \ninterior and west coast areas each afternoon. But in general, expect \nscattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms through basically \nthe whole long term period. Main hazards will be lightning strikes, \ndamaging gusty winds, large hail, and localized flooding. \n\nTemperatures will remain above normals, with highs generally in the \nlow to mid 90s, and morning lows in the mid 70s over inland South FL \nto around 80 closer to the coasts.",
